WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.410
(soft music)

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.400
- [Reporter] Look at this kitchen counter.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.910
So clean.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:05.920
This one?

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.290
Not so much.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:08.890
The first may seem safer,

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:11.660
but both could be
harboring the coronavirus.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.020
- There's a common misconception

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.940
between cleaning and disinfection.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.080
- [Reporter] That's Bryan Warcholek.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.880
He's the general manager of
the COVID-19 response team

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.800
for Aftermath Services.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.940
- Anybody without training can clean.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.877
It's gonna be everything
that you can't see

00:00:24.877 --> 00:00:27.330
that needs to be disinfected.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.080
- [Reporter] He's gonna
explain how to properly

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.033
and safely disinfect your home.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:34.670
- At a bare minimum, you're
gonna want to have gloves

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:37.320
and, potentially, a
paper mask and goggles.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:38.620
When you're preparing to disinfect,

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.100
you're going to need towels,
whether it be paper towels

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.110
or dropcloths or whatever that you have

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:44.370
to wipe the surfaces.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.690
You're gonna need to have
some sort of disinfectant.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:47.670
You're gonna want to assure

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:50.720
that that disinfectant
is on the EPA's list

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:54.030
that specifically kills the coronavirus.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.130
If you can't find any
disinfectants in the store,

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.633
you can make a bleach mixture at home

00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:02.340
using 1/3 cup to a gallon of water

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.670
or four teaspoons to a quart of water.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:06.740
When working with bleach,

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:09.440
make sure that you're in
a well-ventilated area

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:10.790
and wearing the proper PPE.

00:01:12.293 --> 00:01:13.126
(bell dings)

00:01:13.126 --> 00:01:15.563
Step one is going to be
identifying the surfaces

00:01:15.563 --> 00:01:17.260
that you're going to disinfect.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.700
So you're gonna pick out
your high touch areas,

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:23.820
whether it be your light
switches, faucets, doorknobs,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.710
things that you know for
sure you're gonna touch,

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.170
things that you wouldn't even think twice

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.670
when you touch it on normal circumstances

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.900
but you're gonna have to
really have heightened senses

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.950
of what you touch on a regular basis.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.920
And even outside of the home,
there are certain surfaces

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.630
that you probably are
gonna want to disinfect,

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.613
your exterior doorknobs, your mailbox,

00:01:41.613 --> 00:01:44.560
the handle to your
vehicle, things like that

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.860
that you wouldn't normally
think would be high touch

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.175
but you do touch them every day.

00:01:48.175 --> 00:01:49.530
(bell dings)

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:51.680
Step two is going to be
preparing the surface

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:52.800
for disinfection.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.510
Your gonna do this by using the towels

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.820
that you have available with warm water

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.320
or warm, soapy water.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.470
You're gonna wipe down all of the surfaces

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:02.050
that you are going to be disinfecting

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:05.560
to remove any surface
gross filth, dust, debris,

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.100
whatever it may be.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.510
Now that you've cleaned the
surfaces, they may appear clean,

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:12.580
they may appear that
there's nothing there,

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:14.130
but it's gonna be what you can't see,

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.660
the microscopic bacteria, microbials,

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.930
viruses that you can't
see that you have now

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.823
prepared the surface for
the disinfectant to work on.

00:02:22.823 --> 00:02:23.750
(bell dings)

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:26.460
Step three is disinfect your surfaces.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.600
Now that your surfaces
are ready to disinfect,

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:30.340
you're gonna want to don whatever PPE

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:31.610
that you do have available.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:35.790
Gloves, masks, and goggles
would be best for starters,

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:37.640
then once you have on
your PPE and you're ready,

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.590
you're gonna apply the disinfectant.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:41.980
If your disinfectant is
coming through a spray bottle,

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.940
you're gonna want to mist
it onto the surfaces.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:46.340
You're gonna want to make sure
that you are not saturating

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:47.830
or leaving pooling behind.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:49.660
You need the disinfectant to do its job

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.640
and it needs to be misted onto the surface

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.680
and it has to stay damp
for the allotted cure time.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:56.500
If you have a wipe, you're
gonna want to remove it

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.740
from the container that it's
in, apply it to the surfaces

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.240
by wiping back and forth
across the surface.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.050
You're gonna use that wipe
until it starts to dry up

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:07.753
and it's no longer leaving
any disinfectant behind.

00:03:08.605 --> 00:03:09.438
(bell dings)

00:03:09.438 --> 00:03:11.160
Step four is wait.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.860
You're waiting for the
disinfectant to do its job.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.740
It varies by disinfectant.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:18.610
Some can be as soon as instantaneous

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:20.240
and some can be up to 10 minutes.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.000
Always refer to the label
that's on the bottle

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.880
or refer to the CDC's recommendations

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:27.280
for how long to let it cure.

00:03:29.038 --> 00:03:30.060
(bell dings)

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.980
Step five is going to be
to rinse the surfaces.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:34.250
The best practice is gonna be

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:36.710
to fold a kitchen towel into fourths,

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:39.820
damp that towel, and
then use one side of it

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.050
to do one sweeping motion, open it up,

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:43.570
use the other side of it.

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:45.700
That way, you're getting
the most out of that towel

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.220
when you're rinsing the surfaces.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.070
It's best practice that each swipe

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:51.410
is about five to eight feet.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:54.910
If you're using paper towels,
one towel folded in half

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:56.790
should cover the same five to eight feet.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:58.496
After this, the surfaces will air dry

00:03:58.496 --> 00:04:01.280
or you can dry 'em by hand
with another paper towel

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.407
or another towel you have available.

00:04:03.407 --> 00:04:04.240
(bell dings)

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:06.350
After you are completely
done with disinfecting,

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:08.030
you should probably repeat the process

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:09.370
depending on your lifestyle.

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:11.380
If you're in and out of the
home several times a day,

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.530
you're going to want to
do it several times a day.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:14.790
If you work from home,

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.140
twice, three times a day should suffice.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.690
It should be in the morning,
afternoon, and evening though.

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:22.030
I think when people are getting
comfortable in their homes,

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:24.160
they're getting complacent
and they're not thinking

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.240
that they have to disinfect surfaces,

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.400
but you truly don't
understand who has the virus

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:30.630
'cause they could be asymptomatic

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.660
so you do need to take
the extra precaution,

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:36.026
regardless of what the
surface may look like already.

00:04:36.026 --> 00:04:38.055
(soft music)

